00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:20.359
My name is Sep Kamvar. I am a professor in computer science

00:00:20.379 --> 00:00:24.129
and applied math at Stanford. And before that, I was the

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:26.480
engineering lead for personalization at Google. And before

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.349
that, I had a small company called Kaltix, which was a

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:32.429
personalized search engine, which got acquired by Google in

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:34.329
2003.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.669
My name is Seth. I was with GSB '06, but left in '05, and

00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:43.069
left because I co-founded Meebo along with two other co-

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:47.239
founders. And Meebo started as just a web IM client, it was

00:00:47.259 --> 00:00:51.569
like, hey let people get on to AOL or MSN at the time via

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:54.949
the web. We quickly heard from people that they wanted Meebo

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:57.460
in their sites fundamentally to be a social platform in

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.039
their sites. So today Meebo is distributed through thousands

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:04.700
of sites, it's on philly.com or Entertainment Weekly or TMZ

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:07.710
as the social platform in that sites, so it lets users

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.310
connect up with their friends on Facebook or Twitter or

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:13.629
MySpace or whatever kind of friends they want to connect

00:01:13.649 --> 00:01:18.060
with when they go around the web.

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:21.329
My name is Amit Kapur. I run a company called Gravity and we

00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:23.819
focus on personalizing the internet. Before Gravity, I was

00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:28.780
at MySpace, I was the COO there, where I worked up for a lot

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:32.299
of times since it was a start up, helped build up a lot of

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:34.799
socialization, social media on the web. And before that I

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.769
studied mechanical engineering here. So that's my

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:40.179
background, I don't have a business background or anything,

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:44.090
but I really enjoyed my time here and it's nice to be on the

00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:45.359
other side of the classroom.

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:48.849
My name is Babak Pahlavan. I did

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:53.689
[inaudible] Berkeley Computer Science in 2006. I joined

00:01:53.709 --> 00:01:58.010
[inaudible] Stanford. Basically, I was going to do my PhD

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.299
here. It wasn't exactly realized half year through, it wasn'

00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:05.319
t exactly my cup of tea, left in 2008. Started Clever Sense,

00:02:05.319 --> 00:02:10.489
it's a personalization and mission

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:12.439
[inaudible] as it relates to location based services. We

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:17.369
focus on the locals for mobile. And yeah we started from

00:02:17.389 --> 00:02:21.059
2008 to 2010, got founded 2010 and we are powering through

00:02:21.079 --> 00:02:22.079
[inaudible]

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:26.299
Hi and I am the moderator. My name is Howard Hartenbaum. I

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:29.729
work at a firm called August Capital, which is kind of a

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.750
low-key firm, you may not have heard of. But you have

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.250
probably heard of many of the companies that the partners

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.539
there have backed including Seagate, Symantec, Compaq,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.879
Intuit, Microsoft, Atheros, Xylink, Sybase and bunch of

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.949
others. My lucky entrance into the venture business was, I

00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:51.039
put the first money in the Skype and was on their board,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.719
which was really a wild ride. So if you guys have

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:57.539
interesting companies in the future, I am the guy you come

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.549
and talk to. So let's talk about the topic and I have a few

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:05.569
questions and I thought some of them, I will just go down

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:08.569
and some of them, we will just be directed at one particular

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:11.319
person. So on the first question, I will start with Babak

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:14.319
and maybe we will go down the line, which is you know

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.859
companies here are based on the premise that your users are

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:22.559
willing to input their personal data as part of using the

00:03:22.579 --> 00:03:27.909
service and can you explain what types of data that people

00:03:27.929 --> 00:03:30.679
are wiling to share using your services and what's the value

00:03:30.679 --> 00:03:32.789
that you provide in return for them doing that?

00:03:32.789 --> 00:03:33.789
[Informal Talk]

00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:43.679
So part of the good news is that, we are living in a Y

00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:45.949
generation, I mean people are living practically on Facebook

00:03:45.949 --> 00:03:47.949
and Twitter right now. So as far as,

00:03:47.949 --> 00:03:53.319
[inaudible] what kind of data we will be asking for, the way

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.839
our service works, we are analyzing the conversations that

00:03:56.859 --> 00:03:59.859
we are seeing on the web as people are writing reviews and

00:03:59.859 --> 00:04:03.639
whatnot on different particular outlets. So we have a new

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:05.899
web indexing user-generated content, so people can actually

00:04:05.899 --> 00:04:08.849
use our service without they sharing anything with us, once

00:04:08.869 --> 00:04:12.929
we full out launch it. But then they can further share with

00:04:12.949 --> 00:04:16.900
us, we are Facebook Connect, totally opt-in service Facebook

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.110
Connect or image the Foursquare in Twitter. So it's totally

00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:22.879
an opt-in service, so people can share how much that they

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:27.280
want to share. I mean privacy is a big deal thing,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.289
basically, we are not going to build the service, we are not

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:34.139
going to use ourselves, so we believe in absolute matter of

00:04:34.159 --> 00:04:37.430
giving control to users. So if there is going to be a button

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:40.199
on our service that it can delete everything that got with

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:43.199
us and they can see it, so that's how we see it.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:48.120
Well just as some kind of basic background on, I think just

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:52.590
data in general, so I think all of us sort of experienced

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:57.550
this notion of information overload, where over time the

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:00.459
technologies for the creation of content are often competing

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:03.769
with the technologies for the distribution, the discovery of

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:07.240
content. And so what we end up finding is that major

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:10.329
technology companies will come in and create things that

00:05:10.349 --> 00:05:13.370
will filter that content, filter that data for us, so that

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:16.300
we find more relevance within it. And early on, you had

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:18.590
companies create directories then you had companies create

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:22.629
search, ultimately even those types of filters ended up

00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:26.250
failing with the user generated content and social data that

00:05:26.269 --> 00:05:29.519
was entering the stream. And companies that were creating

00:05:29.539 --> 00:05:32.480
social filters like Twitter, Facebook, MySpace and others

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.129
largely actually find great content, find great information

00:05:36.149 --> 00:05:40.399
by using their friends as a social filter. Once again what

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:42.899
we believe is that we are experiencing another phase of

00:05:42.899 --> 00:05:46.040
information overload, where the amount of user generated

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:49.829
content of social data that is available to all of us, it's

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:53.079
accessible, is overwhelming stream. And so what we do at

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:57.189
Gravity is that we listen to users, we observe them, we take

00:05:57.209 --> 00:05:59.949
a look at what you are saying on Twitter, who you are

00:05:59.949 --> 00:06:02.449
following, what they are saying, what you are liking on

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:04.949
Facebook or you are sharing and we build a holistic

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:07.699
understanding of who you are, what you are interested in. So

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:12.420
if you are liking a story about the iPad 2 on TechCrunch or

00:06:12.439 --> 00:06:15.439
if you are checking at a local sushi restaurant or if you

00:06:15.439 --> 00:06:16.939
tweet about, about the surface and

00:06:16.939 --> 00:06:19.180
[inaudible], we can build a really great profile to

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.660
understand who you are. From that, what we end up doing is

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.790
we partner with third parties as a platform, so that they

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.060
can tap into that data and then personalize their

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.699
experiences or adapt them to users that access their site.

00:06:30.719 --> 00:06:34.350
So in the future, I kind of imagine this world where you

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.519
don't go to accessing internet sites or applications where

00:06:37.539 --> 00:06:39.969
they are all one-size-fits-all, like going to see New York

00:06:39.969 --> 00:06:42.969
Times or going to see ESPN, it actually adapts to you and

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:45.719
your interests. So the best content for you starts to hit

00:06:45.719 --> 00:06:48.469
surface to top and some of the noise gets suppressed, so

00:06:48.469 --> 00:06:50.219
that's what we are doing at Gravity.

00:06:50.219 --> 00:06:54.199
So the whole notion of like what data users give and then

00:06:54.219 --> 00:06:56.289
what they get back for, that's interesting because it's

00:06:56.289 --> 00:06:58.829
really hot right now. Everybody is talking about, oh you

00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:01.219
know are people stealing people's data and are they treating

00:07:01.219 --> 00:07:05.819
people's data kind of inappropriately. And so there is a lot

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:09.889
of work kind of amongst standards bodies right now to say,

00:07:09.909 --> 00:07:14.259
hey how should our industry, which is dependent on data,

00:07:14.279 --> 00:07:17.969
like everything we do at some level depends on users giving

00:07:17.989 --> 00:07:21.129
us data and then us making something interesting out of it

00:07:21.149 --> 00:07:24.149
and spitting it back. How should we treat it like, what are

00:07:24.149 --> 00:07:26.899
the opt-out for users and how much should users know about

00:07:26.899 --> 00:07:29.899
kind of what happens to their data, how we are processing it

00:07:29.899 --> 00:07:32.899
and how easily, like I said, should they be able to delete

00:07:32.899 --> 00:07:38.319
it. So one of the things kind of interesting is most users,

00:07:38.339 --> 00:07:42.509
they probably don't care what happens with their data as

00:07:42.529 --> 00:07:45.990
long as they understand what the expectation of that

00:07:46.009 --> 00:07:49.560
application is. So you know that is look at like the

00:07:49.579 --> 00:07:52.079
difference in the evolution to use two companies that are

00:07:52.079 --> 00:07:54.329
not in this panel between Facebook and Twitter, right.

00:07:54.329 --> 00:07:57.790
Twitter started as a very, very public data system, like

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.969
user gives data, the user expects that it's going to the

00:08:00.989 --> 00:08:03.489
public. And Facebook started as a private data system, users

00:08:03.489 --> 00:08:06.239
gives data and they expect that it's going to be private.

00:08:06.239 --> 00:08:09.339
And Facebook has had a lot of difficulty trying to move over

00:08:09.359 --> 00:08:13.079
to making that expectation of privacy, all of a sudden,

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:15.469
become public. Whereas Twitter has had this really easy

00:08:15.489 --> 00:08:17.980
road, because they just created this default expectation

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.850
with users upfront, it's like "hey your data is public and

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.479
that's what the users thought they were getting." So I think

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:27.659
that at the end of the day with the users, it's just about

00:08:27.659 --> 00:08:29.810
like setting expectations, it's like they had expectations

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.009
that the data is public or that it's private and I think it'

00:08:33.009 --> 00:08:35.829
s very hard to switch from private to public, it's much

00:08:35.829 --> 00:08:37.189
easier to develop public to private.

00:08:37.189 --> 00:08:42.250
Yeah I will follow up on what Seth was saying that I think

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:45.730
one really interesting thing about this question is that the

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:50.500
cultural norms around the value that people perceive and the

00:08:50.519 --> 00:08:53.629
risk that people perceive of giving up their data is

00:08:53.649 --> 00:08:56.590
changing dramatically. I mean if you take a look at the mid

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:00.659
to late 90s, there are a lot of people who say, I don't feel

00:09:00.679 --> 00:09:05.330
comfortable giving my credit card to a website and now that'

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.100
s changed and you see that not only with information that

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.350
people are willing to share with the service, but

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.600
information that people are willing to share with the

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.730
public. And this is a really great opportunity I think for

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:22.629
startups, because when companies do come out, they have an

00:09:22.649 --> 00:09:25.689
existing community with existing cultural norms and it's

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:32.200
hard to change that within the community even though the

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:35.610
overarching cultural norms are changing. And so for example,

00:09:35.629 --> 00:09:41.269
Google, when we switched from begin able to have user

00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:43.840
accounts and associate your user history with those

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:50.159
accounts, it was very difficult to do. And we had to do a

00:09:50.179 --> 00:09:52.509
lot of communications and a lot of putting controls into

00:09:52.509 --> 00:09:56.460
place. I think new search engines not launched today will

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.830
launch with the idea of having your search history

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:04.519
associated with your account from the get-go opted out. And

00:10:04.539 --> 00:10:07.019
so I think that there is a really nice opportunity for

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:09.980
startups as these culture norms continue to evolve at this

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.789
rapid pace.

00:10:11.809 --> 00:10:14.970
So, personalization means different things to different

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:18.330
people and I think one of the challenges of having this

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:23.049
discussion is when Amit says personalization versus when

00:10:23.069 --> 00:10:25.189
Babak says personalization might be different things to

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:27.990
them, and it might be different things to you guys as well,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:31.529
so keep that in mind if you have questions at the end when

00:10:31.549 --> 00:10:34.269
we have a question period. So, I will start the next

00:10:34.269 --> 00:10:37.470
question with Amit and if one of the other guys want to jump

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.470
in afterwards, feel free, which is, you know there was a lot

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.220
of talk about social graph and others, more and more talk

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:48.659
about an interest graph out there and so how is the interest

00:10:48.679 --> 00:10:51.539
graph used for the internet and personalization on the

00:10:51.559 --> 00:10:52.559
internet.

00:10:52.559 --> 00:10:56.949
Sure, so my background before was at Myspace in growing that

00:10:56.969 --> 00:10:59.360
company and starting to see where there was an incredible

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.399
amount of data about users mainly, who they were connected

00:11:03.419 --> 00:11:06.110
with and so that was really the premise behind the social

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:09.110
graph as being able to understand who the user is and who

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.620
they are attached to and then understand that way to

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.889
detachment, because we may have hundreds of friends, but

00:11:13.909 --> 00:11:17.080
there is only a few that were very closely associated with.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.169
What the interest graph does is it gets a notion of what we

00:11:20.169 --> 00:11:23.330
are attached to, so what sports do we like, what industries

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:27.449
are we interested in, what locations are we interested in as

00:11:27.469 --> 00:11:30.649
well, and understand the way to detachment that you have to

00:11:30.669 --> 00:11:34.740
those things, those topics of interest. And with the social

00:11:34.759 --> 00:11:38.250
graph, we have seen these unbelievably amazing experiences

00:11:38.269 --> 00:11:40.769
and value that's been unlocked with it, on sites like

00:11:40.769 --> 00:11:43.269
Facebook as well as all the applications that access it.

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:45.490
With the interest graph, we will see similarly these

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:50.429
unbelievably valuable applications of it where as we use a

00:11:50.449 --> 00:11:53.669
mobile app or as we access an internet website by

00:11:53.689 --> 00:11:56.730
understanding who the user is, what their interests are, we

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.299
can actually start to dynamically adapt that site to that

00:11:59.319 --> 00:12:02.689
user. And as a result, you have a really engaging and

00:12:02.709 --> 00:12:05.980
amazing experience, where you see a lot less of the noise

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.250
that you typically see on web pages and a lot more of things

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:12.000
that will actually interest you. So that's how we see an

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.409
interest graph. And one of the things that I think - a

00:12:15.429 --> 00:12:19.939
nuance with what we do, as we actually attach people within

00:12:19.959 --> 00:12:23.649
that graph, so if you look at semantic technology, the way

00:12:23.669 --> 00:12:26.389
that that works, there is an otology which is a dictionary

00:12:26.389 --> 00:12:30.759
of terms that relate to each other. So where Kobe Bryant is

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:34.029
a player for the Lakers which is in NBA team is also a

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:37.120
celebrity in Los Angeles, and so those all exist as notes

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:40.329
within otology anthology. What we do is we actually layer in

00:12:40.349 --> 00:12:43.659
a people graph on top of those interests and those topics,

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:46.909
so we understand what people are interested and then we can

00:12:46.929 --> 00:12:49.429
help you actually traverse through that graph based on

00:12:49.449 --> 00:12:53.990
common interest with friends. So all of us may be interested

00:12:54.009 --> 00:12:56.730
in Stanford football and because I am also interested in the

00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:00.639
iPad2, if you look at your relationship with me and our

00:13:00.659 --> 00:13:02.519
common interest in Stanford football, you can actually

00:13:02.519 --> 00:13:06.750
direct somebody to the iPad2 via those kinds of inferences.

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:10.899
So there is a lot of complexity that goes into it, but it

00:13:10.919 --> 00:13:14.419
just goes to show there is a lot of power within it too or

00:13:14.419 --> 00:13:17.669
as we start to apply that, we will start to actually be able

00:13:17.669 --> 00:13:20.169
to filter all this information that we are once again

00:13:20.169 --> 00:13:22.500
experiencing this overload with on the internet today.

00:13:22.519 --> 00:13:28.240
So I think in the internet industry, there is the buzzword

00:13:28.259 --> 00:13:31.669
at the moment and interest graph is definitely the buzzword

00:13:31.689 --> 00:13:35.649
at the moment. And I also think that when any given person

00:13:35.669 --> 00:13:38.759
in the internet industry says interest graph, they probably

00:13:38.779 --> 00:13:41.039
have no clue what they are talking about, it's probably

00:13:41.039 --> 00:13:43.289
different from the next person next to them, so -

00:13:43.289 --> 00:13:44.289
[Overlapping]

00:13:44.289 --> 00:13:54.509
But I guess I will say that, they just think, like you and

00:13:54.529 --> 00:13:57.029
your personal interest graph and you know what you are

00:13:57.029 --> 00:13:59.029
talking about, but everybody else's definition of interest

00:13:59.029 --> 00:14:02.490
got that it is probably very different in New York. And so

00:14:02.509 --> 00:14:05.740
when you think about, I guess the way I approach it is, you

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.939
think about people's relationship with other people, so it's

00:14:08.959 --> 00:14:11.730
like you have got your very, very close circle of friends.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.360
And you are probably, like you SMS with that,

00:14:15.379 --> 00:14:18.939
[inaudible], you call them. Then you have, you are kind of

00:14:18.959 --> 00:14:21.959
second order friends, it's like the people who you wish you

00:14:21.969 --> 00:14:25.219
cud keep in touch with, but you don't have time to keep in

00:14:25.219 --> 00:14:28.549
touch with. Now a lot of the social stuff in the web today

00:14:28.569 --> 00:14:31.959
has done a really, really good job of keeping you in touch

00:14:31.979 --> 00:14:35.610
with kind of your close friends, and you are now like with

00:14:35.629 --> 00:14:38.689
the advent of Facebook and to something Twitter and

00:14:38.709 --> 00:14:41.189
something that was in general second older friends, really

00:14:41.209 --> 00:14:44.779
good job, you know MySpace is same thing right. But then

00:14:44.799 --> 00:14:48.000
there are all these other people who you probably share

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:51.819
common interest with that you could learn from and you just

00:14:51.839 --> 00:14:55.509
don't have a good way to learn from those people today. And

00:14:55.529 --> 00:14:58.279
if you think about all the social stuff that's really kind

00:14:58.279 --> 00:15:00.889
of default, happened in the web today, it's about your

00:15:00.889 --> 00:15:04.490
friends. But the thing is I am interested like I am a pilot,

00:15:04.509 --> 00:15:07.009
so I am interested in airplanes, and I personally gotten

00:15:07.009 --> 00:15:09.509
into bicycle links, I am interested like you know bicycles.

00:15:09.509 --> 00:15:14.079
The thing is only one of my other friends is a pilot and

00:15:14.099 --> 00:15:18.659
only one of my other friends really just rides bicycles. So

00:15:18.679 --> 00:15:24.120
I can't use my social graph to learn about cool stuff around

00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:28.230
airplanes or bicycles. So what I need to do is somehow find

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:31.809
these other people who I overlap with from an interest

00:15:31.829 --> 00:15:35.509
perspective and learn from them about bicycles and

00:15:35.529 --> 00:15:39.149
airplanes, right. And so I think like in my world of

00:15:39.169 --> 00:15:42.039
interest graph, it's how can you use the fact that like

00:15:42.039 --> 00:15:45.059
maybe you and I are both interested in flying airplanes and

00:15:45.079 --> 00:15:48.259
then how it connect us out there in the internet, then I can

00:15:48.259 --> 00:15:51.720
discover cool content or cool new experiences because of

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:56.220
that. So like Bill Gurley and Max Levchin were recently on a

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:59.649
panel at Goldman Sachs Technology Conference, and they made

00:15:59.669 --> 00:16:02.669
a very similar point, they were kind of like, look there is

00:16:02.669 --> 00:16:05.919
Google all the way over here and like I know I am looking

00:16:05.919 --> 00:16:08.919
for it, it's algorithmic and it's like I want to find out

00:16:08.919 --> 00:16:11.669
about airplanes and an algorithm helps me find it. And there

00:16:11.669 --> 00:16:14.669
is kind of Facebook over here and it's like these are my

00:16:14.669 --> 00:16:17.419
friends and maybe I have some interest over lots of my

00:16:17.419 --> 00:16:20.419
friends like we like the same kind of music, but we really

00:16:20.419 --> 00:16:23.169
don't care and my friends don't care about the same thing,

00:16:23.169 --> 00:16:26.169
like bicycling as I do. There is some middle ground which is

00:16:26.169 --> 00:16:29.529
based on people and based on learning about our interest

00:16:29.549 --> 00:16:34.149
based on people and that has not been done well yet. Like I

00:16:34.169 --> 00:16:37.349
know you are working on it and the ways we are working on

00:16:37.349 --> 00:16:40.599
it, lot of people are trying to figure out how to do that

00:16:40.599 --> 00:16:44.209
middle ground.

00:16:44.229 --> 00:16:45.229
[Informal Talk]

00:16:45.229 --> 00:16:51.879
I want to follow up what Seth was talking about. The

00:16:51.899 --> 00:16:54.549
definition of interest graph, I think changes from, not have

00:16:54.569 --> 00:16:58.100
been just one company to another company, from one person to

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.620
another person. And the interest comes from the fact that

00:17:00.639 --> 00:17:04.339
how interest graph is defined both on the domains that it

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:08.829
covers, let it be from peoples' interests versus items as we

00:17:08.849 --> 00:17:12.599
call it like items can be a place, it can be a brand, it can

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:16.789
be a company. And how these relationships are sit up, it

00:17:16.809 --> 00:17:20.889
will have deep impact on what the interest graph is going to

00:17:20.909 --> 00:17:25.180
be used for, just because somebody mentions on their

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:27.990
Facebook page that I am interested in movies, I mean it

00:17:28.009 --> 00:17:32.099
doesn't mean a whole lot. And just because they say I am

00:17:32.119 --> 00:17:34.369
interested in rock band, doesn't again the definition of

00:17:34.369 --> 00:17:38.129
that changes and how in each graph you talk about, I mean

00:17:38.149 --> 00:17:41.379
literally the reliability of that information, the

00:17:41.399 --> 00:17:43.819
[inaudible] of that information, you could just say that you

00:17:43.819 --> 00:17:47.089
know and I'll say, the inverse of that, actually I don't

00:17:47.109 --> 00:17:50.109
like rock music, I mean how those things are sit up in

00:17:50.109 --> 00:17:52.359
interest graph would actually have implications on how these

00:17:52.359 --> 00:17:57.129
stuff can be used. Talking about notion of personalization

00:17:57.149 --> 00:18:00.450
and interest graph can be used for multiple things, I mean

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:03.220
you can just find information that matters to you, based on

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:08.059
how the graph is set up for a particular entity for a

00:18:08.079 --> 00:18:14.220
particular, let's say brand, like CNN or a place versus how

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:16.430
your friends perceive it, these are completely different

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:19.919
things and I don't know if this is going to be one single

00:18:19.919 --> 00:18:22.169
company who is going to have the overarching encompassing

00:18:22.169 --> 00:18:24.919
more interest, does it all for everybody. I think it is

00:18:24.919 --> 00:18:28.129
much, much more interesting and useful, if you have a focus

00:18:28.149 --> 00:18:31.399
set of graph, that are built for certain things and have a

00:18:31.419 --> 00:18:34.289
very rich and deep understanding of how these relationships

00:18:34.309 --> 00:18:37.639
are set up, so they can come back and use it for a specific

00:18:37.639 --> 00:18:41.329
purpose, because it's just you know how

00:18:41.349 --> 00:18:44.369
[inaudible] originally in old days and it was a counter

00:18:44.389 --> 00:18:47.779
mechanism to find out what the reliability of a particular

00:18:47.799 --> 00:18:52.379
keyword was as it relates to other websites. And same thing

00:18:52.399 --> 00:18:55.750
with the notion of interest graph, to me it's just a lot

00:18:55.769 --> 00:18:59.139
more complex, because these relationships change and these

00:18:59.159 --> 00:19:05.289
relationships are evolving as also people evolve with it. So

00:19:05.309 --> 00:19:08.339
yeah, I mean there are just different definitions of it and

00:19:08.359 --> 00:19:11.569
there are different use cases based on how you set up your

00:19:11.589 --> 00:19:13.990
graph itself.

00:19:14.009 --> 00:19:21.809
Let me agree with Seth on the buzzword aspect and also use

00:19:21.829 --> 00:19:25.490
the basic principle underlying the shift between the social

00:19:25.509 --> 00:19:28.250
graph to the interest graph to help you come up with new

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:33.970
buzzword. So the idea is that we have basically a set of

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:36.740
notes and a set of edges within the notes. And the

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.240
transition from the social graph to the interest graph is

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.970
essentially, you are labeling some of these notes with their

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:45.049
interests. Well you can label them with other things for

00:19:45.069 --> 00:19:47.629
example reputations and so then you have a reputation graph

00:19:47.649 --> 00:19:52.000
or expertise and then you have an expertise graph and then

00:19:52.019 --> 00:19:54.970
you can also do things like label the edges themselves in

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.569
terms of the types of relationship or the type of

00:19:57.589 --> 00:20:01.819
information fuzz along those relationships. And so there is

00:20:01.839 --> 00:20:05.329
benefit around generating new buzzwords, but also to think

00:20:05.349 --> 00:20:08.079
about new kinds of information interaction to happen on

00:20:08.099 --> 00:20:11.750
graphs just using that principle of continuing to further

00:20:11.769 --> 00:20:15.379
label these notes and these edges.

00:20:15.399 --> 00:20:20.079
But with full disclosure Seth is one of the advisors, so we

00:20:20.099 --> 00:20:21.349
learn a lot from him.

00:20:21.349 --> 00:20:22.349
[Informal Talk]

00:20:22.349 --> 00:20:40.109
Actually I tried to be full disclosure, August Capitals and

00:20:40.129 --> 00:20:43.379
investor in Gravity, Amit's company and I tried to be an

00:20:43.399 --> 00:20:46.359
investor in Seth's company and had I known you other guys, I

00:20:46.359 --> 00:20:51.619
probably would have tried to invest in - there you go. So,

00:20:51.639 --> 00:20:56.059
what would the ultimate personalized experience look like?

00:20:56.079 --> 00:20:59.829
What opportunities will emerge when like everything in your

00:20:59.849 --> 00:21:03.359
life is captured within the digital realm? So why don't we

00:21:03.379 --> 00:21:07.899
start with Seth on this one.

00:21:07.919 --> 00:21:10.919
Well, I will answer that question, if you can first tell me

00:21:10.919 --> 00:21:13.309
what the ultimate un-personalized experience will look like.

00:21:13.329 --> 00:21:19.059
Somebody, who doesn't use the Internet.

00:21:19.079 --> 00:21:22.109
So I mean I think that's a very hard question to answer.

00:21:22.109 --> 00:21:26.029
This is a little bit of brave new world like what could it

00:21:26.049 --> 00:21:31.119
be 5 to 10 years from now and maybe you can add into the

00:21:31.139 --> 00:21:34.670
answer like is it too much at that point, is it actually a

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:35.690
bad thing.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:43.089
So I mean I will give a couple of non-answers that answers

00:21:43.109 --> 00:21:49.919
somewhat related questions. I think first is, I think right

00:21:49.939 --> 00:21:55.649
now algorithms for personalization are not very good. I

00:21:55.669 --> 00:22:00.490
think you have essentially two classes of algorithms. One is

00:22:00.509 --> 00:22:02.740
item-based collaborative filtering which is used on things

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:05.490
like Amazon. And the second you have model-based type

00:22:05.509 --> 00:22:10.519
recommendations which I used for a lot of others site,

00:22:10.539 --> 00:22:17.409
presumably with you guys and with you guys. And these

00:22:17.429 --> 00:22:20.950
algorithms have been around for a very long time and there

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:25.169
is a lot of opportunity for new innovations from the

00:22:25.189 --> 00:22:29.049
algorithmic side. And I think when you get those kinds of

00:22:29.069 --> 00:22:31.710
innovations, you'll have much more highly specific type of

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:35.859
recommendations. So, that's one as I think there is room for

00:22:35.879 --> 00:22:38.180
innovation on the outer

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:40.799
[inaudible] side, so that you have a much higher specificity

00:22:40.819 --> 00:22:43.909
and it's the kind of thing that you will note when you see

00:22:43.909 --> 00:22:46.409
it like Google search results were so much better than

00:22:46.409 --> 00:22:48.909
Altavista search results. So I think that's one kind of

00:22:48.909 --> 00:22:55.369
brave new world of personalization. I think second is as

00:22:55.389 --> 00:23:03.519
more kinds of data be moved to the web, you have a lot of

00:23:03.539 --> 00:23:06.269
kinds of data that are not amenable to traditional keyword

00:23:06.289 --> 00:23:09.539
search and a much more amenable to personalized

00:23:09.559 --> 00:23:13.919
recommendations. I mean music being an example, but job

00:23:13.939 --> 00:23:17.190
search being an example. I mean there is some stat like 80%

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:21.829
of people are passively looking for a job, real estate, so

00:23:21.849 --> 00:23:24.809
on. And so, when you combine these two and not to mention

00:23:24.809 --> 00:23:27.460
things like video, for example, where for entertainment

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:30.700
needs, it doesn't make sense to do a keyword based search.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:34.409
So, when you have this combination of better algorithms for

00:23:34.429 --> 00:23:38.190
personalization and new types of data that are much more

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:41.609
amenable for personalization that's I think the two trends

00:23:41.629 --> 00:23:44.980
that will point towards the ultimate personalization

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:48.609
experience. If I knew the specific answer to that, I'd

00:23:48.629 --> 00:23:50.720
probably just started company around that.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:58.389
I think as Seth said, it's pretty difficult, I mean these

00:23:58.409 --> 00:24:00.990
things, we know things are going to get more personalized

00:24:01.009 --> 00:24:03.259
because there is the problem of information overload. As

00:24:03.259 --> 00:24:05.700
Amit was talking about, it's pretty obvious, I mean it's

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:06.700
just, you are living

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.669
[inaudible] as more and more people are sharing and there is

00:24:09.689 --> 00:24:12.349
more and more, you are getting bombarded by information. I

00:24:12.369 --> 00:24:16.019
mean right now, we all, I think live in a society which is

00:24:16.039 --> 00:24:19.139
just ADD-driven, I mean attention deficit disorder is a real

00:24:19.159 --> 00:24:21.690
thing. I mean everyone is just all constant, getting mobile

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:24.710
with Twitter and Facebook and new sites and there is just so

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:27.939
much happening. I think the way we see it at least, I don't

00:24:27.939 --> 00:24:31.460
know if it's going to be 20, 30 or even like years from now.

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:37.139
But one thing that we would like to work toward is that this

00:24:37.159 --> 00:24:40.700
notion of the information fund and you in a right place at

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:46.799
the right time, obviously real-time such that we would

00:24:46.819 --> 00:24:52.119
understand your relationship with things that you care about

00:24:52.139 --> 00:24:56.279
and as they are moving around, as they are changing, if an

00:24:56.299 --> 00:24:59.399
event triggers that would really, really interest you, and

00:24:59.419 --> 00:25:02.859
we would find you and deliver it to you, whether you are on

00:25:02.879 --> 00:25:06.710
the web, whether you are on the go, much like I mean the

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.089
vision would be, I mean somebody who care of you when, you

00:25:10.109 --> 00:25:13.349
are on the bed. You don't need to sift through piles and

00:25:13.349 --> 00:25:17.079
piles of documents, I mean right now I mean most attorneys

00:25:17.099 --> 00:25:19.059
or executives and whatever they have, assistants who

00:25:19.059 --> 00:25:21.579
actually sift through stuff and say, these are the things

00:25:21.599 --> 00:25:24.099
you should pay attention to, these are meetings right here,

00:25:24.099 --> 00:25:26.849
make sure you read this document, I mean that's what they

00:25:26.849 --> 00:25:29.349
do, but they are human beings who sifting through that

00:25:29.349 --> 00:25:31.599
information and they also understand your priorities. So I

00:25:31.599 --> 00:25:34.019
think a personalized role, I mean I will tie around

00:25:34.019 --> 00:25:36.019
[inaudible] personalizing role around you is such that you

00:25:36.019 --> 00:25:37.450
would find information of the

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:40.950
[inaudible] you are on to go and we would actually try to do

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:44.149
an experience of, we are very big on serendipity, I mean it'

00:25:44.169 --> 00:25:47.279
s actually search, so we would actually do think on your

00:25:47.299 --> 00:25:50.240
behalf when you are not thinking and try to deliver

00:25:50.259 --> 00:25:52.960
information as it would relate to you, serendipitously.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:53.980
[Informal Talk]

00:25:53.980 --> 00:26:03.139
I mean just sort of take it a little bit away from being too

00:26:03.159 --> 00:26:06.210
conceptual. I think that there are some things that we are

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.009
seeing today where personalization is actually very

00:26:08.029 --> 00:26:12.470
effective in the today and in near future and then to sort

00:26:12.490 --> 00:26:16.089
of look where we go ahead in a few years. We will talk about

00:26:16.109 --> 00:26:19.169
that in a second too. But even today, I think if you look at

00:26:19.169 --> 00:26:22.409
practical applications of personalization, music is one,

00:26:22.429 --> 00:26:25.490
where it is very effective. So my background, in MySpace, I

00:26:25.509 --> 00:26:28.509
ran MySpace Music when I was there, it was one of my

00:26:28.509 --> 00:26:31.369
responsibilities. And we were part of this revolution where

00:26:31.389 --> 00:26:34.669
music became very social. And I think that before that, you

00:26:34.689 --> 00:26:39.139
saw this, I mean even to take a step back from that for a

00:26:39.159 --> 00:26:41.409
second though, one of the things that personalization does

00:26:41.409 --> 00:26:44.159
very effectively is it helps us sift through a list of

00:26:44.159 --> 00:26:47.039
options. And so, early on, even before social DJs and

00:26:47.059 --> 00:26:49.629
editors would help us sift through a list of options, so

00:26:49.629 --> 00:26:52.929
with the radio DJ or an MTV DJ that's how we discovered

00:26:52.949 --> 00:26:56.250
great music. A new phase happened when social entered the

00:26:56.269 --> 00:26:59.339
scene, so people were either sharing mix tapes or you were

00:26:59.359 --> 00:27:01.859
creating play list and sharing that on MySpace where you

00:27:01.859 --> 00:27:05.789
discover an ever increasing pool of music via your friends

00:27:05.809 --> 00:27:10.230
as a filter for finding better options. And today with

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:14.940
Pandora, there is a product a lot of us used where you

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:17.779
actually are receiving personalized music. And I think what

00:27:17.799 --> 00:27:20.889
they do very effectively is they don't get too caught up by

00:27:20.889 --> 00:27:23.490
going too deep in the sites, because you can very much go

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:26.490
too deep to the point that you are not finding a practical

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:28.990
application things. But what they have done with the music

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.929
genome project is that they used humans to actually

00:27:31.949 --> 00:27:34.669
categorize the content and then they understand based on

00:27:34.689 --> 00:27:37.189
your behavior, what you are listening to, what you are

00:27:37.189 --> 00:27:39.939
liking and disliking, they are able to take you down a

00:27:39.939 --> 00:27:42.189
recommendation path and that is a very, very effective

00:27:42.189 --> 00:27:45.819
personalization experience. Another one which has been used

00:27:45.839 --> 00:27:49.009
on Yahoo today, is their front page, they have a Today unit,

00:27:49.029 --> 00:27:52.629
where they run millions of different permutations and call

00:27:52.649 --> 00:27:55.899
it what you will or it may not be the much advanced science

00:27:55.899 --> 00:27:58.649
behind what they are showing you, even if it's just basic

00:27:58.649 --> 00:28:01.129
demographic data, targeted data or location targeted data.

00:28:01.149 --> 00:28:06.159
But they see 200% to 1,000% uplift an improvement for

00:28:06.179 --> 00:28:09.210
personally targeted versus non-targeted content in that

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:13.690
space. So, what we are doing is similarly, we are trying to

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:16.909
imply that technology where I imagine this future which is

00:28:16.929 --> 00:28:20.419
not too far off where you go to the New York Times or you go

00:28:20.419 --> 00:28:23.919
to ESPN or you go to yahoo.com and every single one of us

00:28:23.919 --> 00:28:26.169
has a slightly different experience that's catered to us,

00:28:26.169 --> 00:28:29.109
based on the likelihood that I live in LA, I like the

00:28:29.109 --> 00:28:33.299
Lakers, I like golf and I like tennis, if there are good

00:28:33.319 --> 00:28:35.639
stories, those should be the ones that are surfaced at the

00:28:35.639 --> 00:28:39.230
top on ESPN for me, instead of the one-size-fits-all just

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:41.730
like you are picking up a newspaper experience that we get

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.359
today. So I think that that's something that's not very far

00:28:44.359 --> 00:28:48.720
off. Way in the future and not even that far off too is I

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:52.259
think where things get very-very heavily dynamically

00:28:52.279 --> 00:28:55.000
personalized, because we start to get smarter and smarter as

00:28:55.019 --> 00:28:59.329
we take these incremental steps. And in some way, every

00:28:59.349 --> 00:29:02.689
story, every link that you see could be somehow waited to

00:29:02.709 --> 00:29:05.209
you and with an understanding of, you are certainly yield

00:29:05.209 --> 00:29:07.529
optimizing to the person, is there a likelihood that they

00:29:07.529 --> 00:29:11.029
are going to want to see this or click on this. And then it

00:29:11.029 --> 00:29:13.779
gets smarter and adapts based on your reactions to that. So

00:29:13.779 --> 00:29:16.279
we should not be seeing the same group-on deals every

00:29:16.279 --> 00:29:19.029
everyday. We should not be seeing the same websites as we

00:29:19.029 --> 00:29:21.769
visit them. The applications that we open up should be

00:29:21.789 --> 00:29:24.970
somehow different personalized to each of us, even if its

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:28.339
basic is the location that we are in with stuff that Babak

00:29:28.359 --> 00:29:31.769
is doing. So, I think that thinking about things at that

00:29:31.789 --> 00:29:34.250
practical level really gets you sense for the power of

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:37.339
personalization in even your turn.

00:29:37.359 --> 00:29:41.829
So something Meebo is working on right now is this notion of

00:29:41.849 --> 00:29:44.179
web check-ins just because we are distributed through so

00:29:44.179 --> 00:29:46.929
many sites, so we want to start letting users track into

